#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204741} :activity/id #uuid "534c3fc9-4831-5fca-ad59-09b388796c6f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049199 :topic/id #uuid "e2d5739b-5d8f-5b44-8b7e-0b4e50252e45" :topic/math-academy-id 4951} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204741 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205291 :tutorial/content "When factoring a trinomial, we should always begin by factoring out any constant factors. Then, we factor whatever quadratic remains using the techniques we've studied.\n\nFor example, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\n6y^2+21y+9\n$$\n\nThe first thing to notice is that all the terms have a common factor of $3$. So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $3$:\n$3(2y^2 + 7y + 3)$\n\nSo, our goal now is to factor the trinomial in parentheses:\n\n$$\n2y^2 + 7y + 3\n$$\n\nTo do this, we find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times 3 = 6$), and\n- add to the linear coefficient ($7$).\n\nAlso, since all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the *positive* factor pairs of $6$.\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $6$ are\n\n$$\n(1,6),\\quad (2,3)\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(1,6)$ add to $7{:}$\n\n$$\n1+6 =7 \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $y+6y$, and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2y^{2} + 7y + 3 = \\\\\n2y^{2} + y + 6y + 3 &=  \\\\\n(2y^{2} + y) + (6y + 3) &=  \\\\\ny(2y + 1) + 3(2y + 1) &=  \\\\\n&= (y + 3)(2y + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6y^{2} + 21y + 9 &=  \\\\\n3(2y^{2} + 7y + 3) &=  \\\\\n&= 3(y + 3)(2y + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nWhenever the coefficient of the quadratic term is negative, we should always factor this out first. That way, we're left with the task of factoring a trinomial containing a positive quadratic coefficient. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "35b6c679-c9c6-5b44-8de5-21106bc70674" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9456 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "97ca27d9-5627-500f-b4ce-94d3d62acc77" :step/math-academy-id 178290 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204742}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204742 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203942 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203744 :question/id #uuid "f57cd9ee-7613-5083-948a-2434078d6796" :question/math-academy-id "e-15071"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf03377-f06d-5831-80b8-dcbf4978a8db" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049023}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204336}] :knowledge-point/title "Quadratics With Positive Coefficients (After Initial Factoring)"} :step/id #uuid "53a8db76-c802-563a-80d3-ee07f4085259" :step/math-academy-id 178291 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204743}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204743 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203943 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203745 :question/id #uuid "c6a7913e-d53e-5885-b30c-87148e86032d" :question/math-academy-id "e-15088"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b49cf980-1078-593b-bf56-c9a132c41273" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204345}] :knowledge-point/title "Quadratics With Negative Constants (After Initial Factoring)"} :step/id #uuid "408a8b32-052b-5f8b-9893-3e4c9e92fd6e" :step/math-academy-id 178295 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204744}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204744 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203944 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203746 :question/id #uuid "81afbe63-1358-5d90-999d-4797f5f44f78" :question/math-academy-id "e-15089"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e1a8957-9964-5eba-b5f4-b0e1dd525838" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204184} {:db/id #edb/ref 175921862